Hospital Research Fund</grant-sponsor> (<grant-num>no. 04-2006-047</grant-num>), the <grant-sponsor>Korea Health 21 R&#x00026;D Project, Ministry of Health &#x00026; Welfare</grant-sponsor> (<grant-num>no. A040042</grant-num>), and the <grant-sponsor>21C Frontier Functional Proteomics Project</grant-sponsor> (<grant-num>no. FPR05C2-010</grant-num>).</p></fn></fn-group></back><floats-wrap><fig id="F1" position="float"><label>Figure 1</label><caption><p>A11084G polymorphism of mtDNA. PCR products digested with BsmI were sized on a 1% TAE agarose gel with a 100-bp DNA ladder marker (lane 1). Lane 2 is an amplified 402-bp mtDNA encompassing position 11,084. Lane 4 is a 600-bp positive control containing the BsmI site at position 13,309-13,314. Lanes 3 and 5 are PCR products digested with BsmI from lanes 2 and 4, respectively. The patterns of the PCR products from migraineurs and control subjects were identical in lanes 2 and 3.</p></caption><graphic xlink:href="jcn-3-127-g001"/></fig><fig id="F2" position="float"><label>Figure 2</label><caption><p>Representative sequencing results of each mtDNA SNP that is possibly related to migraine. Arrows indicate the SNP sites.</p></caption><graphic xlink:href="jcn-3-127-g002"/></fig><table-wrap id="T1" position="float"><label>Table 1</label><caption><p>Possible SNP sites for migraine in mitochondrial genomes. The total number indicates the number of samples with valid gene amplifications and sequencings. Statistical significances (<italic>p</italic> values) between groups were tested using the chi-square test</p></caption><graphic xlink:href="jcn-3-127-i001"/></table-wrap></floats-wrap></article>